.\" Man page for gortt, the GORT canopy reflectance model.
.\" View with:  man ./man/gortt.1
.TH GORTT 1 "2026-09-16" "gortt" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
gortt \- geometric optic radiative transfer model for discontinuous plant canopies
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gortt
.RI [ options ]
<
.I angles.dat
>
.I output.dat
.br
.B gortt
.B \-W
.RI [ options ]
<
.I /dev/null
>
.I probs.dat
.br
.B gortt
.B \-lidar
.RI [ options ]
>
.I waveform.dat
.br
.B gortt
.B \-u
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gortt
is an implementation of the analytical hybrid GORT model of Ni et al. (1999).
It predicts the bidirectional reflectance factor (BRF) of a forest canopy at
optical wavelengths (400\-2500 nm) by treating the crowns as randomly located
ellipsoids and computing the viewed areal proportions of four scene components
together with their reflectance signatures:
.
.TP
.B C
sunlit crown
.TP
.B G
sunlit background (soil)
.TP
.B T
shaded crown
.TP
.B Z
shaded background
.
.PP
The surface reflectance is the sum
.IR "Kc*C + Kg*G + Kt*T + Kz*Z" ,
where the
.I K
terms are the viewed proportions.  The
.I K
terms depend only on geometry; the signatures depend only on wavelength and
the scattering properties of leaves and soil.
.
.PP
Leaf reflectance and transmittance are supplied by PROSPECT\-D, which is linked
in directly and driven by the
.BR \-N ,
.B \-Anth
and
.BI \-C xxx
options.  Soil reflectance comes, by default, from a weighted sum of the four
Price (1990) empirical orthogonal functions; it can instead be a constant or be
read from a file.  Both can be overridden entirely (see
.BR "SPECTRAL OVERRIDES" ).
.
.PP
.B gortt
is a deterministic filter: it reads the view/illumination geometries from
standard input, writes one line of results per geometry to standard output, and
has no other side effects.  All model configuration is on the command line.
.
.PP
Optionally,
.B gortt
will also integrate the BRF over the viewing hemisphere to give a spectral
albedo and a shortwave energy balance (see
.BR \-energy ).
.
.SH INPUT
The first line of standard input is a header:
.
.PP
.RS
.I N M W_1 W_2 ... W_M
.RE
.
.PP
where
.I N
is the number of view/illumination geometries that follow,
.I M
is the number of wavelengths, and
.I W_i
are the wavelengths in nm.  Every wavelength must lie in the range 400\-2500;
anything outside it is a fatal error.
.I M
must be at least 1.  Both counts are checked against what is actually read and a
mismatch is a fatal error.
.
.PP
The header line is echoed verbatim to standard output before any results.
.
.PP
The remaining
.I N
lines are four whitespace\-separated columns of degrees:
.
.PP
.RS
.I view_zenith view_azimuth solar_zenith solar_azimuth
.RE
.
.PP
Only the relative azimuth matters, so the view azimuth is conventionally given
as 0 and the solar azimuth as the relative azimuth.
.
.SH OUTPUT
One line per input geometry, beginning with the four input angles echoed back at
.BR %f ,
followed by the BRF at each of the
.I M
wavelengths in header order.  Additional fields are appended, in this order,
when the corresponding options are given:
.
.TP
.B \-prnspec
after each BRF value, the four component signatures for that wavelength,
enclosed in braces:
.RI "{ " "C G T Z" " }"
.
.TP
.B \-prnprop
after the last wavelength, the four viewed proportions, enclosed in brackets:
.RI "[ " "Kc Kg Kt Kz" " ]"
.
.TP
.B \-energy
at the end of the line, three values per wavelength: the spectral albedo, the
fraction of incident radiation absorbed by the vegetation, and the fraction
absorbed by the soil.
.
.PP
The reflectance and albedo columns are printed with
.BR %f ,
i.e. six decimal places, unless
.B GORTT_PREC
is set (see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ).
The echoed angles are always
.BR %f .
.
.SS Lidar output
.B \-lidar
replaces all of the above.  Nothing is read from standard input and no BRF is
printed; the output is a single waveform, in one of two layouts.
.
.PP
By default the per-layer profile is written: a header line
.RS
.IR "nlayers wavelength " rho_v " " rho_g " " J0 " " Rg
.RE
followed by one line per canopy height layer,
.RS
.IR z " " P(z) " " dP/dz " " Fapp(z) " " Rv(z)
.RE
running top-down, which is the order in which the return arrives.
.I P(z)
is the total gap probability at nadir,
.I Fapp(z)
the apparent foliage profile
.RI "d log " P "/d" z ,
and
.I Rv(z)
the canopy return density.
.I Rg
is on the header line rather than in a data row because the ground return is an
energy while
.I Rv
is an energy density.
.
.PP
With
.B \-lidar_waveform
the canopy and ground returns are instead binned onto a uniform range grid and
convolved with the transmitted pulse, so that both appear on the same footing.
The header line is
.RS
.IR "nbins wavelength " rho_v " " rho_g " " J0 " " binsize " " sigma
.RE
and each of the
.I nbins
following lines is
.RI "\fIz\fR " w(z) ,
again top-down, with
.I w
a density in the same units as
.IR Rv .
The convolution conserves energy exactly, so
.RI "sum(" w ") * " binsize
does not change with
.BR \-lidar_pulse .
.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Crown geometry
There are two mutually exclusive ways to describe the canopy geometry.  The
"old style" options are those of the original GORT papers; the "new style"
options are the parameterisation of Quaife et al. (2008).  They describe the
same thing, but the new style is preferred because it reduces equifinality.
.B As soon as any new style option is given, all old style options are ignored:
.BR \-HB ,
.B \-BR
and
.B \-PCC
cause
.IR r ,
.IR b ,
.IR h1 ,
.I h2
and
.I lambda
to be recomputed at the end of parsing, with
.I r
fixed at 10 m.
.
.TP
.BI \-h1 " arg"
Lower boundary of the crown centres, m.  Default 3.0.
.TP
.BI \-h2 " arg"
Upper boundary of the crown centres, m.  Default 8.5.
.TP
.BI \-b " arg"
Vertical crown radius, m.  Default 2.6999988 (3.55263 * r).
.TP
.BI \-r " arg"
Horizontal crown radius, m.  Default 0.76.
.TP
.BI \-lambda " arg"
Tree stem density, m^\-2.  Default 0.405.
.TP
.BI \-HB " arg"
Ratio of the crown centre height range to the vertical crown radius.
Default 2.0.  Case sensitive.
.TP
.BI \-BR " arg"
Ratio of the vertical to the horizontal crown radius.  Default 1.0.
Case sensitive.
.TP
.BI \-PCC " arg"
Projected crown cover at nadir.  Default 0.5.  Case sensitive.
.TP
.BI \-beta " arg"
Force the proportion of mutual shadowing between crowns to
.IR arg .
If this is not given, the model of Li and Strahler (IGARSS '92) is used to
determine mutual shadowing.
.
.SS Amount of leaf material
.TP
.BI \-favd " arg"
Foliage area volume density within the crown, m^\-1.  Default 0.858.
.TP
.BI \-LAI " arg"
Leaf area index for the scene, m^2 m^\-2.  Default 2.0.  Case sensitive.
.B \-LAI
overrides
.B \-favd
and is converted to it using
.IR lambda ,
.I r
and
.IR b ,
so it is applied after any geometry conversion.
.
.SS PROSPECT-D leaf properties
Defaults are those of the PROSPECT\-D reference driver.
.TP
.BI \-N " arg"
Leaf structure parameter.  Default 1.2.
.TP
.BI \-Cab " arg"
Chlorophyll content, ug cm^\-2.  Default 30.
.TP
.BI \-Car " arg"
Carotenoid content, ug cm^\-2.  Default 10.
.TP
.BI \-Anth " arg"
Anthocyanin content, ug cm^\-2.  Default 1.0.  Also accepted as
.BR \-Canth ,
which was for a long time the only spelling the parser recognised.
PROSPECT\-D itself calls this parameter Anth, without the C ("content")
prefix carried by the other pigments, which is why it is the odd one out.
.TP
.BI \-Cbrown " arg"
Brown pigment content, arbitrary units.  Default 0.0.
.TP
.BI \-Cw " arg"
Equivalent water thickness, cm.  Default 0.015.
.TP
.BI \-Cm " arg"
Leaf mass per unit area, g cm^\-2.  Default 0.009.
.
.SS Price soil spectra
.TP
.BI \-rsl1 " arg"
Weight of the first Price soil vector.  Default 0.2.
.TP
.BI \-rsl2 " arg"
Weight of the second Price soil vector.  Default 0.1.
.TP
.BI \-rsl3 " arg"
Weight of the third Price soil vector.  Default 0.03726.
.TP
.BI \-rsl4 " arg"
Weight of the fourth Price soil vector.  Default \-0.002426.
.
.SS Spectral overrides
.TP
.BI \-refl_leaf " arg"
Set the leaf reflectance to the constant
.IR arg ,
bypassing PROSPECT.  Must be given together with
.BR \-trans_leaf .
.TP
.BI \-trans_leaf " arg"
Set the leaf transmittance to the constant
.IR arg ,
bypassing PROSPECT.  Must be given together with
.BR \-refl_leaf .
.PP
The two are a pair because the radiative transfer needs both separately: the
single-scattering albedo is their sum, but the scattering asymmetry is built
from their difference,
.RB ( r \- t )
(see
.B gortt_rt_init
in
.IR gortt_brdf.c ).
Giving one without the other is a fatal error rather than defaulting the
missing one to zero, which would quietly change the single-scattering albedo.
These replace the older
.BR \-alb_leaf ,
which set reflectance and transmittance to half its value each and so could
only ever describe a symmetric leaf, pinning the asymmetry to zero:
.B \-alb_leaf 0.4
is now
.BR "\-refl_leaf 0.2 \-trans_leaf 0.2" .
.TP
.BI \-alb_soil " arg"
Set the soil albedo to the constant
.IR arg ,
bypassing the Price EOFs.
.TP
.BI \-soil_spectra " file"
Read the soil reflectance spectrum from
.IR file ,
bypassing the Price EOFs.  See
.BR FILES .
.PP
.B \-alb_soil
and
.B \-soil_spectra
are mutually exclusive; whichever appears last on the command line wins.
.
.SS Gap probabilities
Computing the gap probabilities is the expensive part of a run, and they depend
only on the canopy and crown geometry.  They can therefore be computed once and
reused across many spectral variations.
.
.TP
.B \-W
Compute the gap probabilities, write them to standard output, and exit.
Standard input is never read.
.TP
.BI \-P " file"
Read gap probabilities from
.IR file ,
as written by a previous
.B \-W
run, instead of computing them.
.PP
The geometry options must be identical between the
.B \-W
and
.B \-P
runs.  This is
.B not
checked: giving different geometry silently produces wrong answers.  Only the
spectral options (PROSPECT, Price and the overrides above) may vary between them.
.
.SS Output and mode switches
.TP
.B \-prnspec
Print the component signatures for each wavelength; see
.BR OUTPUT .
.TP
.B \-prnprop
Print the viewed proportions of the scene components; see
.BR OUTPUT .
.TP
.B \-energy
Compute and print the spectral albedo and the fractions absorbed by vegetation
and by soil; see
.BR OUTPUT .
This integrates the BRF over the viewing hemisphere and so is substantially
more expensive than a plain BRF run.
.TP
.B \-q08_pn_kopen
Use the Lewis functional approximation to the gap probability terms, as used in
Quaife et al. (2008), in place of the full numerical treatment.  This is much
faster, but it is only coded for h=0 and should be used with caution when the
crown centres span a range of heights.
.TP
.BI \-diffuse " frac"
Set the fraction of incident radiation that is diffuse to
.IR frac ,
i.e. fix the direct fraction at
.RI 1\- frac .
Without this option the direct fraction is derived per geometry from the solar
zenith angle following Ni et al. (1999).  Not listed by
.BR \-u .
.TP
.B \-u
Print a brief usage message to standard error and exit successfully.
.
.SS Numerical resolution
.TP
.BI \-nlayers " n"
Use
.I n
canopy height layers, default 15, minimum 2.  This is the vertical resolution
of the whole model, not only of the lidar: it sets the layer thickness
.IR dz ,
the path\-length bin width
.I ds
used by the within\-crown gap probability integral, and hence the BRF as well.
Raising it is expensive \(em the gap probability tables grow faster than
linearly \(em and for lidar mode it is the dominant cost.
.
.SS Lidar
.TP
.B \-lidar
Predict the lidar waveform instead of the BRF, then exit.
.IP
Lidar mode does not read standard input at all.  The device is assumed to be at
nadir, so no view/illumination geometry is meaningful, and
.B gortt
short\-circuits in the same way
.B \-W
does: it computes the gap probabilities, writes one waveform and returns.  The
normal BRF output is suppressed entirely.
.IP
The model is that of Ni\-Meister et al. (2001).  The gap probabilities are
needed at every canopy layer rather than at the ground only, so a lidar run is
an order of magnitude slower than a BRF run at the same
.BR \-nlayers .
.IP
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-W ,
.B \-P
or
.BR \-q08_pn_kopen ;
see
.B CAVEATS
below.
.TP
.BI \-lidar_wl " nm"
Lidar wavelength in nm, default 1064 (the VCL, LVIS and SLICER line).  Must lie
within 400\-2500.  The leaf and soil optics at this wavelength come from
PROSPECT\-D and the Price soil model exactly as they do for the BRF, so
.BR \-Cab ,
.BR \-alb_soil ,
.B \-soil_spectra
and the rest all apply.
.TP
.BI \-lidar_J0 " x"
Emitted pulse energy, default 1.  It multiplies both the canopy and the ground
return, so the default makes the output a fraction of the emitted energy.
.TP
.B \-lidar_waveform
Emit the range\-binned, pulse\-convolved waveform rather than the per\-layer
profile.  See
.B Lidar output
above for the two layouts.
.TP
.BI \-lidar_pulse " m"
Gaussian pulse width (one standard deviation) in metres, default 0, meaning no
convolution.  Applies to
.B \-lidar_waveform
only.
.TP
.BI \-lidar_binsize " m"
Range bin size in metres.  The default is the canopy layer thickness
.IR dz ,
which depends on the geometry and on
.BR \-nlayers .
Applies to
.B \-lidar_waveform
only.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GORTT_PREC
Number of significant digits used to print the reflectance, component and
albedo columns.  Must be between 1 and 17; 17 round\-trips a double exactly.
If unset, those columns are printed as
.BR %f ,
which is about four significant figures for a typical reflectance, and is too
coarse to see a small numerical change.  Intended for regression testing, which
is why it is an environment variable rather than an option.  Out\-of\-range
values are a fatal error.
.
.SH FILES
.SS Gap probability files
Written by
.B \-W
and read by
.BR \-P .
Plain ASCII, one record per line.  Records with a non\-negative first field are
.RI "\(dq" "index P(n=0) epgap" "\(dq"
for that zenith index; the single record with an index of \-1 carries
.I k_open
and
.IR k_openep .
Values are written at full precision
.RB ( %.17g ).
.
.SS Soil spectrum files
Read by
.BR "\-soil_spectra" .
Two whitespace\-separated columns per line, wavelength in nm and reflectance,
in ascending order of wavelength.  The file must span the whole model range:
the first wavelength must be <= 400 and the last >= 2500.  The grid need not be
regular; the spectrum is resampled onto a 1 nm grid by linear interpolation and
then interpolated to the requested wavelengths.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Any error: an unknown or incomplete option, a malformed header or angle line, a
count on the header line that does not match what was read, a wavelength
outside 400\-2500, a file that cannot be opened, or a soil spectrum file that
does not cover the model range.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
BRF at four wavelengths for one view/sun geometry, for a canopy with an LAI
of 4:
.
.PP
.RS
.EX
echo \-en "1 4 450 600 800 1000\en10 0 30 20\en" | gortt \-LAI 4.0
.EE
.RE
.
.PP
The same, but printing the component spectra, the viewed proportions and the
energy balance terms:
.
.PP
.RS
.EX
echo \-en "1 2 550 860\en0 0 30 0\en" | \e
    gortt \-LAI 3.0 \-prnspec \-prnprop \-energy
.EE
.RE
.
.PP
Compute the gap probabilities once, then reuse them for a chlorophyll sweep.
Note that the geometry options are repeated identically on every run and that
only the spectral options change:
.
.PP
.RS
.EX
gortt \-LAI 4.0 \-W < /dev/null > probs.dat
for cab in 20 40 60 ; do
    echo \-en "1 1 800\en10 0 30 20\en" | \e
        gortt \-LAI 4.0 \-P probs.dat \-Cab $cab
done
.EE
.RE
.
.PP
A canopy described in the Quaife et al. (2008) parameterisation, at full output
precision:
.
.PP
.RS
.EX
GORTT_PREC=17 gortt \-HB 2.5 \-BR 1.5 \-PCC 0.6 \-LAI 4.0 < angles.dat
.EE
.RE
.
.PP
The lidar waveform of a canopy, as a per\-layer profile.  Note that there is no
input redirection: lidar mode does not read standard input.
.
.PP
.RS
.EX
gortt \-lidar \-LAI 4.0 > profile.dat
.EE
.RE
.
.PP
The same canopy as an instrument would record it, at 1064 nm with a 1 m pulse
and 15 cm range bins, on a fine vertical grid:
.
.PP
.RS
.EX
gortt \-lidar \-lidar_waveform \-lidar_pulse 1.0 \-lidar_binsize 0.15 \e
      \-nlayers 60 \-LAI 4.0 > waveform.dat
.EE
.RE
.
.PP
The tree geometry of Ni\-Meister et al. (2001), table I, case B, which their
figure 4 plots:
.
.PP
.RS
.EX
gortt \-lidar \-r 1.2 \-b 3.6 \-lambda 0.24 \-h1 6 \-h2 12 \-favd 0.44 \e
      \-nlayers 40
.EE
.RE
.
.SH CAVEATS
.SS Option matching
Options are matched by case\-insensitive prefix over a fixed number of
characters, in the order they appear in the parser, so
.B abbreviations are not safe
and a shorter option can shadow a longer one.  In particular
.B \-b
and
.B \-r
are matched last and at two characters, so a mistyped
.B \-be
is silently taken as
.B \-b
and consumes the following value as the vertical crown radius.  Always spell
options out in full.
.B \-HB ,
.BR \-BR ,
.B \-PCC
and
.B \-LAI
are case
.IR sensitive ;
the rest are not.
.PP
Two orderings in the chain are load\-bearing and worth knowing about.
.B \-nlayers
is tested first, because
.B \-N
is matched case\-insensitively at two characters and would otherwise swallow it
as the PROSPECT structure parameter.  All of the
.BI \-lidar_ *
options are tested before
.BR \-lidar ,
which is matched at six characters and would otherwise swallow all of them.
.
.SS Mutually exclusive options
.B \-lidar
is refused, with a message on standard error and a non\-zero exit status, in
combination with any of
.BR \-W ,
.B \-P
or
.BR \-q08_pn_kopen .
All three deal only with the gap probabilities at the ground: the
.B \-W
file format stores that one row, and the Q08 functional approximation is only
derived for it.  A waveform is a function of height and needs every layer, so
the alternative to refusing would be to return zeros for the whole canopy
above the ground.
.PP
This means a lidar run cannot reuse a cached
.B \-P
probability file, and must recompute the probabilities every time.
.
.SS Grazing angles
Both the direct path and
.B \-P
return
.B \-nan
at a view or solar zenith of exactly 90 degrees, because the secant of 90
degrees is infinite.  The gap probability tables are clamped to 89 degrees to
keep them finite, but the BRF path is not.  Avoid the endpoint.
.
.SS Leaf angle distribution
The leaf angle distribution is effectively hardwired to a spherical
(G = 0.5) distribution.  Requesting anything else produces a warning and falls
back to 0.5.
.
.SS Lambertian background
The energy balance assumes a Lambertian background, and the
.B \-energy
path reads the background signatures back out of the scene components after the
hemispherical integral has overwritten them.  This is only safe because neither
background component depends on view direction.
.
.SS Known numerical issues
Two known discrepancies against the published equations are deliberately left
in place so that any fix shows up as a reviewable change in the regression
goldens: the last trapezium panel of the k_open integral is built from a zero,
and the principal\-plane interpolation in the sunlit crown kernel carries a
factor of F squared where the analytic branch carries F.  See
.I OPEN\-QUESTIONS.md
in the source distribution.
.
